From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 07:17:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] Add b-roll compositing for the spokesperson-with-overlay
 format
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The "expert explains something while related footage plays above him" format:
one locked-off take of a person talking for the whole runtime, with
illustrative footage blended into the upper part of the frame and crossfading
between beats. Scene detection on a reference of this format finds almost
nothing, because the picture never cuts — everything that changes is this
overlay layer.

Three decisions worth stating:

The overlay is one continuous track, not N independent overlays. Building it
as a single xfade chain and compositing once gives true dissolves between
beats and one pass over the frame. Overlaying each beat separately washes the
transition — at the midpoint both layers are semi-transparent over the plate,
so you see the base bleed through — and costs a pass per beat.

Gaps are transparent filler segments, not absences. The format deliberately
drops the overlay near the end so the presenter lands the closing line
unassisted, and xfade has no concept of a hole, so a hole is expressed as a
fully transparent clip the chain dissolves to.

The bottom edge is feathered through the alpha channel, per beat, at that
beat's own opacity. A hard-edged box reads as picture-in-picture; the feather
is what makes it read as one image. Alpha comes from a greyscale ramp merged
in with alphamerge, rasterised through sharp — the same path the watermark
already uses. geq would do the same job by evaluating a per-pixel expression
on every frame, which is far slower for a result that never changes.

planBeatTrack is pure and exhaustively tested, because chained xfade offsets
are the kind of arithmetic that looks right and renders wrong: each join
shortens the timeline by one crossfade, so offsets accumulate against the
running total rather than the original beat times. It also clamps a crossfade
longer than the segment it joins rather than failing — the caller's timings
are intent, the transition length is taste.

The compositor is tested by encoding real video and reading pixels back with
sharp: overlay present during a beat, plate visible in a gap, the feather
actually darkening toward the bottom edge, per-beat opacity landing, short
footage looping to fill its slot, and the plate's own audio surviving.

23 tests. Geometry defaults measured from a reference: overlay across the top
45% of frame, bottom 18% of the box feathered, 700ms dissolves.

