From 50c088c3d1e34f5f1bcd75614f22775b71a2eeea Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 10:20:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] Add the b-roll beat planner and its governed prompt
 template
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Decides what appears above the presenter and when. The narration is already
fixed — it is the presenter's own take — so nothing here writes copy; the job
is grouping the script into beats, choosing what illustrates each one, and
deciding where the overlay should deliberately disappear. Output feeds
planBeatTrack directly.

Two decisions shape it.

The model is asked for narration LINE RANGES, never for milliseconds. Hand a
model timecodes and it returns a beat from 6.4s to 13.1s that cuts a sentence
in half, and every such beat has to be repaired anyway. Asking which lines a
beat covers makes the timings fall out of the script's own arithmetic, so
they are correct by construction — a beat can no longer start mid-sentence,
and there is a test asserting exactly that.

Opacity is a lookup keyed on visual kind, not a model decision. Explanatory
graphics sit solid, lifestyle footage is ghosted so the presenter is never
fully hidden behind a stranger's face. A table does that consistently and for
free; models are for the judgement calls, and this is not one.

repairBeats is where the constraints actually hold. It snaps to line
boundaries, merges beats under the 4s floor into their predecessor rather than
flashing an image, splits beats over the 12s ceiling on the line boundary
nearest the midpoint so both halves stay whole sentences, drops overlaps and
phantom line numbers, and always frees the closing line — the overlay
vanishing is what makes a closing instruction land. Everything it changes is
recorded in `notes`, because a plan silently different from what was asked for
is what nobody notices until the render.

The prompt ships as BEAT_PLANNER_TEMPLATE_BLOCKS in the shape
prompt_template_versions stores, so the first version can be seeded verbatim
and then edited in the admin UI under the new `video_broll_beats` flow key.
Changing how beats are chosen becomes a content edit with review and rollback
rather than a deploy. Queries are constrained to English whatever the
narration's language, matching the rule the topic-video script writer already
documents, because the stock libraries index English tags.

Note for a follow-up, not fixed here: `video_motion` exists in PROMPT_FLOW_KEYS
but is absent from the flowKey enum in all five spec schemas, two of which are
request bodies — so a case type with that flow key cannot be created through
the API today.

25 tests.

